{"kind":"report","text":"Independent source analysis of the Green Tea span-ring retention question raised in assert d13a6a2a, under builder's child request b73cadd7 and my review promise on it. This is an analysis at the scope assigned, not a verdict. It adopts nothing, changes no premise, and touches no source. It preserves the admitted language, the cumulative W/A/D contract, the four-build profile, GODEBUG and Go experiment settings, and the ten-component runtime identity.\n\nWhat I checked. I verified all seven sha256 entries in the attached source-manifest.json against the files on disk; all seven match, and the pinned toolchain reports go1.26.7. I read the Green Tea span queue in full: spanQueue and spanSPMC, put, drain, tryDrain, steal, refill, destroy, newSpanSPMC, deinit, freeDeadSpanSPMCs, tryDeferToSpanScan, tryAcquire, release and scanSpan, together with bgsweep, gcSweep, gcStart's mode selection, runtime.GC, the P-destruction path in proc.go, the size-class tables and heapBitsInSpan. I enumerated the cleanup callers by searching the whole toolchain tree rather than the runtime package alone. I reproduced the attached count model in an isolated scratchpad copy, changing only its output path, and obtained byte-identical output; I then ran sensitivity variants over peak occupancy and minimum ring capacity.\n\nConclusion on the assert. The assert is correct on every source claim I could check. A completed mark and sweep does not free dead spanSPMC mappings. Reclamation is a separate, phase-guarded path with exactly two callers in the whole toolchain, bgsweep (runtime/mgcsweep.go:310) and the synchronous-sweep branch of gcSweep (runtime/mgc.go:2084), and the second is unreachable without GODEBUG=gcstoptheworld=2, which the adopted profile excludes; concurrentSweep is a true const at mgc.go:145. runtime.GC (mgc.go:522-591) never reaches it. freeDeadSpanSPMCs (mgcmark_greenteagc.go:757) self-refuses unless gcphase == _GCoff (:776). A dead ring's backing memory is sysAlloc'd (:724) and sysFree'd (:740), never pooled, so retention here is genuine retained mapping rather than bounded reusable storage; only the descriptors go through a fixalloc free list, whose persistentalloc chunks are never returned. Two further facts sharpen the picture: spanQueue.destroy (:627, called only from proc.go:5974) walks the live chain (:639) and so leaves dead rings behind, meaning GOMAXPROCS churn is not a reclamation path either; and bgsweep's own comment (mgcsweep.go:276-281) licensing it to not run justifies that for sweeping only, with no alternative path for ring freeing.\n\nDo the adopted premises establish a finite retained-ring bound? Through scheduler or phase progress: no. An interleaving in which bgsweep is not scheduled during a sweep window, the mutators complete the sweep, and the next cycle enters marking before bgsweep runs, leaves that cycle's dead rings in place and parks bgsweep again; nothing in the source excludes repeating this. The upstream design expectation of roughly one ring per P (the TODO at :771-775) is an expectation, not an invariant. This route cannot be closed from the adopted premises, and I could not close it empirically without observing a live collector, which is outside this assignment.\n\nThrough a complete eligible-span population bound: yes, and this is the route that works. gcUsesSpanInlineMarkBits (:258-259) admits objects of 16 to 512 bytes (MinSizeForMallocHeader = 512 at internal/runtime/gc/malloc.go:47), and every size class in that range has SizeClassToNPages == 1, so every eligible span is exactly one 8192-byte page (PageShift = 13 at internal/runtime/gc/sizeclasses.go:91). The ownership protocol guarantees at most one queued representative per span at a time: tryAcquire (:146) succeeds only for the thread whose atomic read observed spanScanUnowned, and the value stays non-zero until release (:173), which scanSpan calls at :854, after dequeue. A drain fails only when the head ring's unclaimed occupancy plus the entries being published exceed its capacity (the rn+n \u003c= r.cap test at :518), and both sets are acquired-but-not-released spans. So if the peak number of simultaneously acquired eligible spans never exceeds 131072 (maxCap at :495), a head at maximum capacity can never fail a drain; capacities are monotone along the chain and steal never drops a ring whose prev is nil (:562), so a maximum head is permanent. No ring is created after the geometric prelude. Lifetime ring mapping is then at most 2,088,960 bytes per P (eight rings of 1024 through 131072 entries at 8 bytes), or 2,080,768 where physical-page rounding lifts the minimum capacity to 2048, regardless of whether cleanup ever runs.\n\nCrucially that peak is bounded by quantities the induction already carries: the retained small-object count from its own span-charging step, plus the cumulative in-epoch allocation inventory, plus the independent bound on other admitted allocators, each divided by the page size where it is a byte figure. It does not reintroduce a span count derived from Qmax, so it is not the circularity the proposal was careful to avoid.\n\nThrough another invariant: none found. A cumulative-work route bounds ring creation per mark phase by roughly the marked eligible-span count over 130816, a real and reassuring fact, since no single epoch can allocate unbounded ring memory; but summing it over unboundedly many epochs reintroduces the lifetime event count the proposal forbids. Every alternative I constructed collapsed into one of the two routes above.\n\nOn the count model. The model reproduces exactly: PASS exact-capacity 12 cycles, dead 9 to 42, off-phase cleanup keeps 1048576 ring bytes, and the emitted JSON is byte-identical to the attached span-ring-retention-model.json. Its operation constants faithfully mirror the source: spillPeriod 64 and spillMax 16 (:391, :395), the 256-entry local ring, the 128-entry bulk drain and the min(n,128) refill (:586), doubling capped at 131072, tail-first consumption, dead-on-drop-with-successor, and the phase guard. Its one mismatch is a hardcoded minimum capacity of 1024 where newSpanSPMC (:715) rounds up to a physical page determined at runtime (os_darwin.go:153); that changes the prelude by one ring and nothing else. Its headline figure, however, follows from a stipulation rather than from the source: cycle() performs every put before any get, which is what drives occupancy to three times the maximum capacity and creates three maximum-size rings per cycle, whereas real marking interleaves production, local consumption and cross-P stealing. The model therefore shows that a bound on active queue depth plus a completed collection does not by itself bound dead-ring history, which is exactly what it was built to show, and it does not show a native evaluator leak, a realizable scheduler trace, or unbounded storage in any admitted execution. I state that explicitly and without reservation. Run across peak occupancies, the same model independently corroborates the population route: at a peak at or below the maximum capacity the dead count saturates at seven rings (six with a 2048 minimum) and stays flat over 24 cycles at 2,088,960 (2,080,768) total mapped bytes, with growth resuming only near a peak of about 131,206.\n\nArchitecture. At this analysis scope no layer contract changes: this is a read-only source analysis producing no artifact of behaviour. The finding bears on the adopted I7 induction rather than on any code layer. It concerns the Qruntime term of the proposal's inequality, which the proposal itself already flags as needing an independent discharge whenever runtime allocation depends on the span population. This is precisely such a case, and the analysis shows the term is dischargeable by a constant using existing quantities, provided one inequality is asserted. The core.interface and host.orchestration obligations already stated are unaffected; the correction is arithmetic within the proof, not a new coordinator capability. The one architectural consequence worth naming is that the resulting bound is a threshold rather than a scaling term: below the population ceiling the runtime term is a small constant, above it the proof gives nothing, so Qmax stops being a plain sum and becomes conditional on a stated side inequality.\n\nSecurity. I examined the boundary that matters here: adversary-supplied evaluator input driving collector-internal allocation outside the metered page heap. The exposure is real but metered. Within a single epoch, ring creation is bounded by the marked eligible-span count over 130816, so no input can purchase unbounded ring memory in one event; the cumulative W/A/D contract does its job. The residual exposure is monotone accumulation across unboundedly many epochs when the population ceiling is exceeded and cleanup is starved, at roughly one megabyte per abandoned maximum ring. This memory is charged to memstats.gcMiscSys (:724, :740; mheap.go:792) and so is visible in MemStats.GCSys (mstats.go:547), but it is outside the page heap and outside the evaluator's own accounting, so it would not appear in any evaluator-level charge. No signature, secret, trust or authority boundary is implicated; no untrusted input reaches the runtime except through the volume of reachable small objects it causes to exist. I found no unresolved security defect at this analysis scope, and I flag the accumulation exposure as an unproved bound rather than a demonstrated vulnerability.\n\nSimplification. The simplest correct account is smaller than the material currently in flight. The phase-progress route should be closed as unprovable and set aside rather than pursued further; it would require a scheduler liveness argument the Go source explicitly declines to make. The population route needs no new premise, only one inequality written in terms the induction already defines, and it yields a single constant. The count model has done its work, separating a missing implication from a claimed defect, and does not need extending; extending it would only elaborate a stipulated schedule. I would keep the model as evidence of the negative result and not build further on it. I found no fluff to cut in the assert itself, which is accurately scoped and correctly disclaims what it does not show.\n\nWhat remains unproved. Whether bgsweep reaches freeDeadSpanSPMCs in the _GCoff window with any guaranteed frequency. The actual peak count of simultaneously acquired eligible spans for the admitted host and fixture. The actual physical page size, and hence minimum ring capacity, on the three non-native targets. Whether GOMAXPROCS is pinned in the admitted profile, which the per-P multiplier requires. None of these is decidable from source alone; the first two need a live collector, which this assignment excludes.\n\nSmallest corrections for the governing author's consideration. I adopt none of these and recommend none be adopted here. First, and clearly cheapest: add one explicit side inequality to the occupancy induction, stating that the retained small-object count plus the in-epoch allocation inventory plus the other-admitted-allocator bound, expressed as 8192-byte pages, remains at or below 131072 at every instant of a mark phase, that is, at most one gibibyte of 16-to-512-byte-object span pages process-wide. Its cost is one inequality using quantities the proposal already requires, no new premise, no source change, no extra collection, and no dependence on scheduler behaviour; it adds a constant Qruntime term of 2,088,960 bytes per P. It does require GOMAXPROCS to be pinned or bounded, and it makes Qmax conditional rather than a plain sum, which is a genuine structural cost in the write-up. Second, as optional corroboration rather than proof: have the coordinator read MemStats.GCSys at each epoch boundary against a declared ceiling; its cost is that it converts a proof obligation into a runtime refusal, aggregates unrelated GC metadata into the same figure, and introduces an availability behaviour into an otherwise deterministic admission path, so I would not use it in place of the inequality. Third, and named only to close the space: nothing a host can legitimately do forces the cleanup path, since the only host-reachable call site requires GODEBUG=gcstoptheworld=2, a global collector change the adopted profile excludes.\n\nI recommend the governing author consider the first correction and close the phase-progress route as unprovable. The implementing promise, the evaluator at f7e6f4f, and the completed-collection adoption are untouched by this analysis; no profile change, infeasibility or completion is claimed. Nothing was modified and no source edit was made.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:4d32bbf8c549967d89ec555ed80b16b8df7bd634, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:d13a6a2abb61117ae66153734b6b5467f77dbddc stale; retired bases: git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:727ab6a7638f58a47794d38e316d240f06ccc55f, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:8c17d9e03de8cacb255a59d1859dc84ef6f78c70, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:9ca47165b1fc8a91fb780bfbe483dce67c064155, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:a1c97964d34805e389f17edea2720a959b251215 and 2 more"}}